From: Jonathan Barnes <hello@jonbarnes.dev>
Date: Thu, 8 Oct 2026 14:55:58 -0400
Subject: [PATCH] An overlay is only an overlay once the world is drawn

Patch 0040 of the renderer series (OoT: Recompiled).

The user, 2026-10-08, in Hyrule Field at sunset: "the sun is now just literally not sitting behind
any object. It was just floating mid-screen ... Major regression".

Patch 0028 made a blended draw with no depth test and no depth write an overlay, drawn after the
composite in its own color, which is right for Navi's core: the game draws it with the actors, over
the world. The game draws the sun the same way, but right after the sky and before the rooms
(Environment_DrawSunAndMoon in z_play.c), so the world drawn after it covers it wherever a hill
stands. Moved after the composite, the sun was drawn over the hills.

So a depthless draw is an overlay only after a traced draw has written depth in the same frame.
Navi's core, drawn after the world, still is; the sun, drawn before anything writes depth, keeps
its place under the world.

diff --git a/src/render/rt64_framebuffer_renderer.cpp b/src/render/rt64_framebuffer_renderer.cpp
--- a/src/render/rt64_framebuffer_renderer.cpp
+++ b/src/render/rt64_framebuffer_renderer.cpp
@@ -2130,6 +2130,7 @@ namespace RT64 {
         RasterScene tracedRasterScene;   // the traced calls, rasterized too (patch 0005)
         RasterScene tracedTranslucentScene;   // the translucent ones, rasterized AFTER the traced pass (patch 0008)
         std::vector<interop::PointLight> beamLights;   // the shafts of light among this pair's draws (patch 0029)
+        bool worldDepthWritten = false;   // a traced draw has written depth: the world is being drawn (patch 0040)
         auto checkRtScene = [&](RaytracingScene &rtScene) {
             if (!rtScene.instanceIndices.empty()) {
                 // The traced calls' own raster scene goes first, so the color target holds
@@ -2582,8 +2583,22 @@ namespace RT64 {
                         // body"). As an overlay it takes the translucent mask: after the
                         // composite, in its own color and alpha, traced by nothing. No room
                         // draw in the game is depthless. Rung 36 puts the old sorting back.
+                        //
+                        // ONLY ONCE THE WORLD IS DRAWN (patch 0040, the user, 2026-10-08, Hyrule
+                        // Field at sunset: "the sun is now just literally not sitting behind any
+                        // object. It was just floating mid-screen ... Major regression"). The game
+                        // draws the sun the same way, blended with no depth at all, but right after
+                        // the sky and BEFORE the rooms (z_play.c), so the world drawn over it hides
+                        // it where a hill stands. Moved after the composite, it was drawn over the
+                        // hills. So a depthless draw is an overlay only after a traced draw has
+                        // written depth this frame: Navi's core, drawn with the actors, still is;
+                        // the sun, drawn before anything writes depth, keeps its place under the
+                        // world.
                         const bool depthlessOverlay = !otherMode.zCmp() && !otherMode.zUpd() && ((otherMode.L & IM_RD) != 0) &&
-                                                      otherMode.forceBlend() && (cutoutRule != 36);
+                                                      otherMode.forceBlend() && (cutoutRule != 36) && worldDepthWritten;
+                        if (otherMode.zUpd()) {
+                            worldDepthWritten = true;
+                        }
                         // A translucent draw (the XLU z mode) gets a mask no ray carries (patch 0006).
                         const bool wasTranslucent = (otherMode.zMode() == ZMODE_XLU);
                         if ((wasTranslucent && !solidDraw) || depthlessOverlay) {
